WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.417
"All’s quiet now," the doctor said. "I’ll see to the wounded."

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:09.644
"Jim and I will make something to eat," the squire volunteered.

3
00:00:09.645 --> 00:00:16.399
We cooked outside in the hot sun, trying not to listen to the loud groans from the doctor’s patients.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:26.996
Of the eight men who had fallen in the action, only three still breathed: Hunter, Captain Smollett, and one of the pirates shot by the doctor.

5
00:00:26.997 --> 00:00:34.844
The pirate died within hours. Hunter never regained consciousness and died the following day. The captain was luckier.

6
00:00:34.845 --> 00:00:41.218
"Job Anderson’s bullet broke your shoulder blade and nicked your lung, but not badly," the doctor told him.

7
00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.442
"The second bullet tore some calf muscles, but you’ll recover.

8
00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:49.783
For the next few weeks, don’t walk or move your arm if you can help it."

9
00:00:49.784 --> 00:00:55.249
After eating, the squire and the doctor sat and talked beside the captain for a while.

10
00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:02.527
A little past noon, Dr. Livesey grabbed his hat and pistols, strapped on a cutlass, and put the map in his pocket.

11
00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:09.542
With a musket over his shoulder, he crossed the north side of the fence and set off briskly through the trees.

12
00:01:09.543 --> 00:01:16.091
Gray took his pipe out of his mouth when he saw the doctor go and said, "Is Dr. Livesey mad?"

13
00:01:16.092 --> 00:01:20.611
"No," I replied. "He’s the sanest person I know."

14
00:01:20.612 --> 00:01:28.436
"Well," Gray said, "if he’s not insane, then I am! I just saw him climb over the stockade."

15
00:01:28.437 --> 00:01:31.697
"He’s probably going to see Ben Gunn," I said.

16
00:01:31.698 --> 00:01:35.271
"It’s too bad Mr. Gunn wants people to ‘come alone.’

17
00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:40.107
The doctor is enjoying the cool woods, while we’re roasting inside this stockade!

18
00:01:40.108 --> 00:01:44.931
I hate the stink of blood and all these dead bodies around!"

19
00:01:44.932 --> 00:01:48.659
"Help me wash out the house," Gray said.

20
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:55.106
We scrubbed the blood off the floor, and I also washed the cookware, but my disgust only grew stronger.

21
00:01:55.107 --> 00:01:58.550
I wanted to leave, but I knew they wouldn’t let me go.

22
00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:02.938
So I decided to wait and slip out when nobody was watching.

23
00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:05.589
But I needed a good reason to leave my friends.

24
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.719
"I’ll go find Ben Gunn’s boat," I told myself.

25
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:16.859
I filled my pockets with biscuits so I wouldn’t be hungry, and I took two pistols, gunpowder, and some bullets.

26
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:23.488
While the squire and Gray helped the captain with his bandages, I bolted over the fence and into the trees.

27
00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:27.326
Before anyone noticed, I was far away.

28
00:02:27.327 --> 00:02:34.879
"I’ll head east, so the pirates won’t see me from the anchorage, and then I’ll go south," I thought as I snuck through the tall woods.

29
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:41.528
By late afternoon, I had come to the cool beach and was walking alongside the tumbling surf.

30
00:02:41.529 --> 00:02:50.977
When I was far enough south, I took cover in thick bushes and crept warily up a ridge near a bit of sandy land sticking out from the shore.

31
00:02:50.978 --> 00:02:57.591
I was right between the sea and the anchorage, which was protected from the force of the ocean by Skeleton Island.

32
00:02:57.592 --> 00:03:06.532
The Hispaniola was still and quiet, floating on an unbroken mirror of water, with the Jolly Roger hanging from her mast.

33
00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:14.287
I saw a familiar pirate sitting in a shore boat alongside the ship, talking and laughing with two men leaning over the ship’s stern.

34
00:03:14.288 --> 00:03:17.446
"Long John Silver!" I whispered to myself.

35
00:03:17.447 --> 00:03:26.795
They were much too far away for me to overhear or see them clearly, but all at once, I heard the most horrid, unearthly screaming.

36
00:03:26.796 --> 00:03:31.062
"ARGH! ARGH!"

37
00:03:31.063 --> 00:03:39.395
"What’s that?" I thought, startled. But then I spotted the bright plumage of a bird perching on Long John’s wrist.

38
00:03:39.396 --> 00:03:42.472
"Oh, it’s just the parrot!" I sighed.

39
00:03:42.473 --> 00:03:45.918
Long John shoved off in the boat and rowed for shore.

40
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:52.609
The two other pirates—one was the man with the red cap—stayed on the ship and went below.

41
00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:58.189
The sun set behind Spyglass Hill, fog rose, and the light began to fade.

42
00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:04.040
"I must hurry if I’m going to find Ben Gunn’s boat," I told myself. "It’s going to get dark soon."

43
00:04:04.041 --> 00:04:12.778
The white rock, visible above the trees, was about two hundred yards away, and I crawled on all fours among the bushes.

44
00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:20.968
By the time I touched the rock’s rough sides, the area was hard to see clearly, but right below in a ditch, was Ben Gunn’s boat.

45
00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:26.579
It was a very small, crooked structure of tough wood, covered with stretched goat skin.

46
00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.613
"I can’t imagine a full-sized man in this," I muttered.

47
00:04:30.614 --> 00:04:37.674
There was one low seat, a kind of stretcher in the front, and a double paddle for propulsion.

48
00:04:37.675 --> 00:04:45.787
I had never seen such a boat; it looked as if it had been made by some ancient seaman, but it was light and portable.

49
00:04:45.788 --> 00:04:52.195
After having found Ben Gunn’s boat, I should have returned to the stockade, but suddenly I had a great idea.

50
00:04:52.196 --> 00:04:57.143
"I’ll cut the Hispaniola adrift!" I said to myself.

51
00:04:57.144 --> 00:05:02.821
The pirates, after the morning’s disastrous attack, might pull up the anchor and sail away.

52
00:05:02.822 --> 00:05:07.359
"Long John left his men on the ship without a shore boat," I thought.

53
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.614
"It’ll be easy. The fog is covering everything.

54
00:05:10.615 --> 00:05:16.254
Tonight will be perfect for my plan!" I ate my biscuit and waited for dark.

55
00:05:16.255 --> 00:05:23.868
As daylight disappeared and absolute blackness settled over Treasure Island, I picked up Ben Gunn’s boat.

56
00:05:23.869 --> 00:05:29.113
Only two points were visible on the landscape: one was the great fire,

57
00:05:29.114 --> 00:05:37.860
where the pirates were drinking in the swamp, and the other was a mere blur of light in the dark fog—the anchored ship’s cabin window.

58
00:05:37.861 --> 00:05:43.940
The tide was out, and I sank in swampy sand to my ankles before I got to the water’s edge.

59
00:05:43.941 --> 00:05:53.759
Wading in, and with some strength and skill, I set Ben Gunn’s boat in the water.

